{"docId":"87a35ded-e127-4928-9a29-ae7a4f790388","title":"Worg Quest","author":"anthony robert hunter","content":"<h1 data-scene-id=\"9e095d8b-4b20-46eb-b9fd-cb6b6cdfd66b\">Day 1, Ben - Down, down, down</h1><p><br></p>The billowing of the ship's sails reminded me of home, when mother would shake out my bedsheets and wake me for school. That is a sentence I once dreamed of writing. Florid and poetic. A romancier's memory. But this is not that kind of ship, and I never had no mother.&nbsp;<p><br></p><p>There were a thousand people on this journey traveling not north or south, but down, down, down into the earth in a vessel made of the new science. No sails or rudders or crow's nests from which to spy an island and shout, 'land ho!' All of my pirate fantasies died the moment I saw the massive column of steel and brass and smoke already plunged half-deep into the ground. No lands to see and no windows to see them through. There had been a drawbridge opened up from the middle of the leviathan like a great tongue, and people marching up into its maw, side by side, willingly offering our bodies to this horrible thing.&nbsp;A crier stood at the platform, holding a scroll in her hand and shouting half-speech and half-spittle as we passed.</p><p><br></p><p>\"Come you all to slay an undergod!\" she screamed. \"Ten thousand skulls to kill the Warg. Come skullmaidens and skullmages alike. That monster lives still in the caves of hell, while we are bereft of our savior - Fil - Overlord of Molgerlund. Taken in battle, leaving the great Overlady alone to defend us. She calls for the head of the beast. Answer! Ten thousand skulls it buys you, by promise of the imprimatur of the Molgerhead.\"</p><p><br></p><p>It was all I had needed to hear: ten thousand skulls. That was enough to buy me a ship to anywhere, beyond Molgerlund and its rain and swamps and wormworthy food. I, Benthic, not of this house or that, no skullmage or maiden, but still here, questing among professional questlords for the great prize of our day. A life beyond the reach of the past and its shackles. Within my grip, it was.&nbsp;</p><p><i><br></i></p><p><i>Was</i>.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"Bendick! Bendick the beauty! Get your white cheeks up on the grindwheel and spin.\"</p><p><br></p><p><i>Bendick</i>. That was what the halfmaster called me now, and had since the moment I set foot on the ship. Well, not foot, but wheels. You see, I did not walk up that gangway with the rest of the adventurers. The crier was not crying out to me with her pleas and promises. I was one of a hundred dragged up the rear entry, straight into the leviathan's arse, chained in a chainbox pulled by pig oxen.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"Got a hundred pound of coal for your smashing, Bendick,\" the Halfmaster Papin said to me. \"You like smashing, Bendick, you do. Smashing coal, smashing lockboxes, all the same.\"</p><p><br></p><p>It was a lie. I hated smashing lockboxes. The wooden ones splintered and covered the skulls and gemry with wood dust, and the metal ones just flattened out when you hit them without opening. It was a brute's method, and lockpicking was hardly better with all the tooling it took and time it wasted. No, I preferred the craft of seduction. There always was a halfmaid or princeling in need of a good afternoon nap, and me just the right rogue to tire them for it. Low-made, but high-boned and handsome was Benthic of Bhorne.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"Bendick!\" Papin yelled.</p><p><br></p><p>I pulled myself up and ran the chain ring along the floor rail. There were a dozen iron rails running in every direction, each for a different function. Latrines, kitchens, meal service. But my own led straight to the furnaces because, for a thief, I was too strong to be wasted on the easy work. Hard labor was my life, and, if honest, my profession. Because, again, if honest, Benthic of Bhorne, like he had with piracy, only aspired to thievery. And seduction. In truth, my first attempt at talking a butcher's wife into bed so I could steal a pork shank went cockwise fast, landing me a cleaver's scar across the jaw and these shackles around my feet. No grand career of masterminding and roguishness for me, yet. <i>Yet</i>.</p><p><br></p><p>So what is Benthic? Who am I? In this world - not Molgerlund, or its environs, but the grand Overskull itself - I am no one. Not even Benthic, a name I gave myself. I am one man, at the least, born in Bhorne - or so I was told. There is one definition of me that is true beyond all else.</p><p><br></p><p>\"Bendick! Stop waddling and get your hand on that hammer. This digger won't dig itself.\"</p><p><br></p><p>I, Ben - you can call me Ben - am an aspirer. I will climb, and climb until I reach a height proportional to my lowish origins. But first I must go down, down, down to the Underskull. And slay the great Warg, a mountaintall beast, to earn my ten thousand skulls. And also, my freedom. Actually, I'll need that first. Legally, even an attempted thief is still a criminal and therefore cannot claim the Molgerhead bounty. Details, only details.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"I better see you swinging, Bendick,\" the halfmaster warned, and I heeded it.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>I place my promise here on the pages of this stained Worg. The hammer I take in my hand will smash not only coal, but a life of insignificance. I go down, but will return higher. This quest will give me my name.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Will you join me?</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h1 data-scene-id=\"f6174bbb-a0f3-48fc-9e0d-83f23a518e12\">Day 1, Heresy - Kill me yesterday</h1><p><br></p><p>Father took my Worg today. Said I spend too much time putting my thoughts into \"adolescent screeds.\" That I would waste this whole experience in ill-rooted, girlish notioning. But I am a girl, and so it would be hard to not have a girlish notion. Should I strive against my nature, and what? Think a thing that only a man would think. What do men think about - perhaps their wankrod? But that makes me unladylike to think on. And if I think about my own womanly body as a man would do, then I am only thinking of that same fundament which develops those girlish notions. This: the folly of men's advice, writ in their daughters' cerebral ink.</p><p><br></p><p>\"Heresy,\" he said to me. \"You will waste your life in that thing, forging fantasies. I gave up my Worg when I gave up my mother's milk. Your mother herself only kept one so long so that she could pass it down to you. I'm sorry to take it, but you must focus on your life in the true world.\"&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>But poor Father does not know that I stopped using Mum's old red rose-enscribed Worg when I was still in mind and body a girl. I am a skullmaiden now, trained in the tutellage of skullmages, who need no paper and quill to manifest their heartmind. The cantrips are known to me, and though I must practice their incantations still, I expunge my thoughts at my leisure.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p><i>The mind and the heart needs be separate, </i>say the skullmages in their homilies<i>. Heartmind is chaos. It must out.</i>&nbsp;<i>Our Worg is the beast that consumes the heartmind, and in its belly churns the chaos into form.&nbsp;</i></p><p><i><br></i></p><p>Chaos, I think, would be an improvement on the world. I sit here in my room, well-apportioned it may be, with a black velvet sofa at the foot of my double-queen bed, and I put my ill feelings into that ethereal paper the mages call memory. This is not a time I care to remember, though. How sour it is for me to be here on the great legendmaker journey of our days, in a burrowing metal mole filled with hundreds of desperate skullseekers and only one true slayer, and to be irreverent of its importance.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>And I do want to care about this quest. After all, I was bred for questing. Father famously undertook the journey to Mount Cathmar and extingushed the great fire that threatened half the Overskull, and Mother ate of the poisonroot tree to distill its elixir and save the Prince of Midgarlund. Statues of my ancestors litter the streets of cities I've never even seen. Their feats are common lore, and I am the last of them. Me, Heresy of Lark, born to slay, and my sword feels nothing but dull.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>You see, it isn't the weight of those ancestral legacies that bothers me. Father always says I'm too anxious about proving something to history, but I'm not. I hear of great deeds and cannot distingish them from goatmilking, and I hate goat cheese. There is a malaise in my heartmind that I have not been able to cure, in spite of trying, as I tried today.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>There was a sound outside of my door sometime early this morning. I was in that kind of half-sleep that I've been limited to since the first night spent in this overgrown shovel, and my room was starkly unlit to try and compensate for the lack of true nighttime. A thin orange light outlined my oaken doorway from the gaslamps in the hall, but the light was interrupted by movement. Shadows flashed quickly as if someone was running back and forth. The sound of the footsteps was inconsistent, some light and some heavy, and I heard the suggestion of an argument in mumbles through the doorway. I arose and dressed decently, then considered whether to take my sword. The malaise took the decision and questioned, pokingly, why I always thought I'd come up against a real swordfight. I left the blade.</p><p><br></p><p>Then I pushed through the doors with enough strength to rattle the walls. The abrupt sound had the desired effect and I watched four little men rush down the hallway and out of sight, all of them dragging something hefty between them. I lost sight of them around a bend in the passage but followed quickly. My feet were bare and touched the cool carpeted floor. It was of a fine material in this part of the ship, though I knew that if you kept walking the circumference of the hallway until you reached the other side of the subterraner's belly, the carpet would be multitudes cheaper. Not carpet at all, really. Whatever stuff they used in the working section had already worn away from the footwork and showed the metal underneath. But not here where my footfalls were silent.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>I rounded a corner and stopped. The four men had halted behind a doorway set back in the wall which offered a bit of cover. They were huddled and chimmering between each other, mouths knashing at their words in a fever. The two facing away from me showed their shaven heads tattooed in red ink, both depicting scythes with grinning smiles for the blades.&nbsp;<i>Skullreapers. Kill me yesterday.&nbsp;</i>They must have been desperate to come all the way round to the mageclass quarters and risk reaping someone here. Desperate or dumb.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"You don't need to skin 'im,\" I heard one of them squeal. \"Just start at the mouth and peel.\"</p><p><br></p><p>Dumb was the answer.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>I surprised the two facing away from me by grabbing their necks and lifting them up, but my hands slipped on their oily skin and they both dropped to the floor.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"Lords, dont you 'reapers ever bathe?\" I yelled, wiping my hands on one of the wall tapestries.&nbsp;They all four attempted to scurry away, but not before I moved in front of them and blocked their path. Behind them in the doorway was a thick canvas blanket with something moving underneath it.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"Boys, a skullmaker!\" one of them laughed, and they all joined in sniggering. \"We're all spoken for here, miss. No need for a night's work so early in the morning.\"</p><p><br></p><p>I did some smirking, then remembered the sleepcloth I was wearing. It painted a picture, but still. A lady of the night? Could they really not know what I am?&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"Do you meet many skullmakers that are three times your height, boys?\" I asked, kneeling down and pulling my black hair away from my neck. \"My mark is not so easily spotted as yours.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"Damned we are,\" said one.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"Three heads,\" said another.</p><p><br></p><p>\"Impaled on a claymore,\" said the third.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>And the fourth quickly shouted. \"What the hell is a skullmaiden doing dressed in her bedsheets?\"</p><p><br></p><p>I winked. \"To defy expectation. And what are four skullreapers doing on such fine carpets as these? And what wriggles beneath their blanket?\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"Nothing that should bother a 'maiden,\" one said.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>There was a sound from underneath the blanket. High pitched and painridden. One of the 'reapers kicked at the blanket with his tiny boot, so I grabbed his oily head and threw him behind me into the hall.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"Hey!\" one of them shouted. \"This is private business. You're not the law. We weren't bothering you!\"&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"You got me out of bed,\" I replied and, pushing the rest of the 'reapers out of the way, lifted the canvas blanket. There was something that looked like a hunter's cloak, a big pile of green and brown fur, soaked in something so sharply odorous it stung the back of my throat. I moved away, and the fur moved too. Large yellow eyes turned to look at me, and then a mouth full of sharp, blood-dribbled teeth opened beneath them. The creature lunged towards me, snapping at my hand. I grabbed it by the neck and felt cold fur atop a shivering body. The animal whined and looked at me with sorrow. I pulled my hand away from its neck and saw that my palm was stained green.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"Did you idiots steal a 'jaeger's hound?\" I said. \"And paint it fucking green?\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"Paint? That's a werg, 'maiden. Found it lurking near the, uh, near the drilling cone. It was hiding there. Plotting, waiting to meet with its master down below.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"It's pronounced <i>varg</i>, 'reaper, and you clearly haven't seen the drilling cone, or else you would have chosen a better lie.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"Well, you haven't seen it either. Who's heard of a 'maiden going down below the upper levels.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"You've heard the wrong stories. I walked this entire ship yesterday when we arrived. All but the drilling cone.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"See! You wouldn't know.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"No one would. I didn't walk the drilling cone because its machinery is entirely sunk in the dirt. There is no passage there, except for one small tunnel barely large enough for a skullwright. Not even big enough for you, and certainly not for this hound. So what are you doing with him?\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"We already said its a private business, this.\"</p><p><br></p><p>I stood fast and knocked the 'reaper's head against the doorframe. He fell and pulled a tapestry off the wall as he did.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"Get to it,\" I said.</p><p><br></p><p>\"Fine, fine. You're right, it's some hunter's dog. The fool left his door open and the dog came loping out looking for food, so we grabbed him up for safe keeping.\"</p><p><br></p><p>I kicked him in the ribs. \"Who are you selling the dog to?\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"Sell him! You're joking. What good's a hunting dog in the Underskull anyway? Nothing to shoot there but rocks. Why would we waste our time selling it?\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"There's a skull price for everything,\" I said. \"I'm guessing that you stole the dog, painted it green to try and pass it off as a warg, and then what? Were you selling it to the 'dancers?\"&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"I'd rather die than get a skull from them,\" the little 'reaper said, then put a proud grin on his face. \"Our customer was a skullmage.\"</p><p><br></p><p>I sighed. \"You are more stupid than I thought.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"No! We're smart! There's a 'mage buying up all kinds of bones and guts off any sort of underlike thing.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"What's their name, this 'mage? I know each of them on this ship.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"We don't know a name.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"People aren't speaking of a name, just a place.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"He goes down by the prisons and waits there for you. And, and you bring him the things, and, and, you get a skull, or many skulls.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"Of course,\" I said, \"the 'mage with no name. That should have been your first clue, fellows. Did you know 'mages make their own names? It's written in their bone ink. They cannot unspeak it, for if they do it burns a hole in their skull like a firepoke. So if this were a 'mage, then their name would be known. But it is not a mage.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"It is! People have seen him.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"And even <i>if </i>it were a mage,\" I explained. \"He would not accept this animal, fools. If you could somehow convince him that it was a warg, no 'mage can make use of a corpse taken in fear like this. If you'd killed it outright, maybe, but you've made this thing suffer. Its soul is broken. No 'mage would pay half a socket for this.\"</p><p><br></p><p>Three of the 'reapers slapped the other one on the back. \"Idiot! We told you it was a stupid idea. Spent the whole night on this and got nothing to show for it.\"&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>I watched them fight for a moment, then heard the dog whimper softly. It was looking at me. Still darting its eyes from left to right in fear every few seconds, but watching me closely.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"I'll buy the dog,\" I said, and the 'reapers stopped fighting.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"What?\" one of them asked in surprise.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"A skull for the dog,\" I said, \"and a skull to tell me where you found it.\"</p><p><br></p><p>They exchanged funny glances. \"But you, you, you could just take the dog, 'maiden. You're bigger than all of us smashed together. We don't understand.\"</p><p><br></p><p>\"There is a skull price for everything, even a good deed.\"</p><p><br></p><p>I ordered them to carry the dog back to my room, which they did, and all parties seemed to begrudge it, especially the dog. A copper-red coin I gave them each, and an extra for them all to split. They scurried away quickly, chortling between them as if they had gotten it over on me. In some views, they had. My father would have surely said so. He would have told me to leave the animal there and call the guard. But there was something in the pup's eyes. Like it was expecting me.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>I'm thinking about that now as I bathe him and try to get the paint off. Surely I'll get a question from the maid about the green-stained tub and the splashes of watery paint on the walls. Where is he from? The green is washing out to a tan fur, wiry and curled. A briarhound. <i>Of course he's a fucking briarhound</i>.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>\"Thought I was through with your lot,\" I tell him, and he looks at me coldly at first, then opens his mouth and gives me a rotten-smelling lick. \"Don't get familiar. Once the world is awake we're getting you back to your 'jaeger. He'll be curious to know where you've been.\"&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>I'll be curious, too. A skulljaeger ought to be tearing the ship apart looking for their creedbound animal.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>The dog whimpers, then stands up and shakes out his fur, spraying me with greenish water.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p><i>Kill me yesterday.&nbsp;</i></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h1 data-scene-id=\"ca5a1814-5c16-4148-89c2-67a81130c310\">Vermon - What's the deal with these Skullmages?</h1><p><br></p><p>The cleaver is sharp, but the cheese is sharper. No, no, no. It's more like, what's sharper than the slice of a cleaver? A slice of cheese. No, there's something else to it. I think it's the type of cheese has to be right, because not all of cheese is sharp. I mean, none of it is <i>sharp</i>. Can't cut anything with a bite of brie no matter how thin you slice it. It's about the flavor. If the cheese is extra cheese-like then that means it's sharper. That's why it's funny, because it has nothing to do with cutting things. That's humor, Mr. Longfry says, two things that don't belong together, but fit perfectly somehow. Cleavers and cheddar.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>That's it! Cheddar is the cheese. What slices sharper than a cleaver? A slice of cheddarly cheese. Yes! I've got to write that one down. But it needs to be broken apart so I say it write. What <i>slice-is</i> sharper than a cleaver? I need to say it like that so that it's got that double meaning Mr. Longry talks about. Damn this tongue of mine. Too late to add that. I'm on in three minutes but I need to go over it all again. Mr. Longry said that I've got to stop winging these things. My anxiety won't be cured by just avoiding it. I need to recognize the problem, make a plan, and follow the plan. Bad things don't disappear just because you ignore them. Instead they grow and grow until they eat you later on.</p><p><br></p><p>Damn. I just looked out through the drapes - not drapes, curtains - and the room is full. Shouldn't have done that. There are three 'mages out there in the front row just glaring. That's the worst. Mr. Longfry says that 'mages don't drink and don't laugh on the outside, if they laugh at all. Why'd they have to come to my show? This is going to be awful. The 'mages won't laugh, and everyone else will be afraid to upset their saintly selves so they won't laugh either. It'll be a dead house.</p><p><br></p><p>No. No, it will not. I just need to tailor my material to the audience. That's the third rule of Longfry's thirty rules to being a 'cracker. Serve dinner on your audience's favorite plate. Or, serve their favorite meal for dinner, but with a plate you designed. Something like that.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Damn. What kind of plates do skullmages eat on? Some kind of unpolished stone, if I remember. I met a 'mage once in an abbey house back home in Hornlund. He was living there doing some odd kind of work that they do, making that smelly ink and drawing all day. Me, I was floorwashing the place at night after they had their messy rituals. Got all too familiar with blood and bone, enough for a lifetime. And so one day this 'mage, said his name was Skulliton, came to me thinking I was the skullery maid or something, asking me where his soup bowl was at. I was getting started in my jestmaking and asked him - oh, what was it? - I asked him, 'Mr. Skulliton, what kind of soup do skullmages drink? Bone broth!' and the fucker didn't even blink. I ain't got nothing even close to that tonight. A dead house.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>I need it to fly tonight. Mr. Longfry said he would be in the audience and taking assessments for his next class at Mandenburg College in Oglund.&nbsp; As soon as we get back up the Overskull, that's my chance. Typically he would have a class running now, too, but he got called up for questing like all these others. Not me, of course. I'm not questing on any Warg slaying. That would have been the joke of all days. 'How goes you, friend, I'll be tunneling to the Underskull with you all and killing demons.' They would have sent me off the side of the gangway. But as a floorwasher, I can serve. No joke there. And so when I was scrubbing the classroom after Mr. Longry's students got out - some of them like to use mess-making objects in their skullcracking, like balloons and such, not to my flavor - I overhear him talking about this quest on a subterraner, sounding all like something out of a 'maiden's tale. Old Vermon thinks, now that's a way to get Mr. Longry on his own, right? A journey under the soil, with nowhere to go.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Okay. The 'reaper is just about to get off the stage. Do you think he'll wonder where his little buddy went off to? I have him tucked between the boxes under that stairwell behind the theater. He's just sleeping, I think, although I hit him a little hard. Tried offering him some skulls for his slot on the stage tonight, but he got it in his 'reaper head that he could swindle me. Shouldn't have hit him that second time. He was so loud! I didn't know what else to do.</p><p><br></p><p>Focus, Vermon. Practice your set-up. That's what Longfry always tells his students: if you can't remember the punchline, just start the set-up and the rest will flow out of you. So, what's my set-up?</p><p><br></p><p>What's the deal with these Skullmages, huh? Something, something. I bet they wish they made better Skullwages. Right?&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>This is going to go bad.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h1 data-scene-id=\"022685d1-21ed-4ae6-bfb4-4c2e99ce63eb\">Cretinus of Dredst - A Warg is not a Worg</h1><p><br></p><p>Let me tell you what a giant subterraner tearing through dirt and rock all day and night does not sound like: <i>progress</i>. Instead to my ears it is the sound of getting farther away, turn by turn of that damned cone, from my beloved. She is waiting there for me - I can see her in my heart - on the shores of Seraphelin's Wake. Her shoulders are high and covered in their stone armor. I hear the sea wind whistling off their sharp parapets. Her back slowly cascades down in those emerald green locks. Her face is shadowed beneath the afternoon sun, but she smiles at me. I know that her bosom is warm and will welcome me on my return. My love. My fortress of Pagina. My great library.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>She is the only thought that could keep a skullmage sane on this fool quest. Slay the Warg? It would take a thousand metal moles like this to even have half the army you would need, but instead of bothering myself with that, right now I am redesigning the third section of Pagina's fifth wing where I hope to be able to fit an additional set of volumes on the Macolomus warriors and their food lore. Did you know that the Macolomus warriors ate the boiled entrails of their slain enemies because they believed them to be the manifestation of their god Placolomus the Overserpent? And that they ate snow eagles stuffed with baked sand eagle eggs and prayed it would moderate their core temperature? And that they never would touch any food fried in dog fat for fear of the Great Warg, even though a Warg is not a dog? Though one cannot blame them for being confused. Ancient and modern peoples alike still cannot understand that a Warg is not a dog.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>This is a problem that I have been using to settle my mind. It is well documented in the annotations of the third edition of the Overbook of Meaning that the Warg is not a direct ancestor to the common house dog. Though their latinized name would suggest otherwise, canis rattus is entirely unrelated to canus lupus familiaris, and in fact to canis lupus at all. A Warg is not a wolf, either. The Warg are indigenous to the Underskull and were named by the ignorant explorers who first discovered them. To their eyes they saw a large quadripedal animal with a tail, wiry brown fur, a sharp elongated muzzle, pointed ears, and fanged teeth. Nevermind the glowing red eyes, the forked tongue, and the thick furless tail. I should forgive them their mistake. The annals say it is dark in the Underskull, but to confuse a rodent with a dog is baffling.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>For the Warg lifted itself up from the common rat some handful of eons ago. Like a great conqueror annexing territory, it claimed all inventory of possible acceleration in the rat bloodline. Whatever was possible in that animal, the Warg exploited it and pushed it to its extreme. This is what all things do. Rats would surely succeed at it in the Overskull if they had been for any amount of time free from their chief predator. But cats cannot survive in the Underskull. There is no water but what is collected from the condensate of steam. There are therefore no fish.&nbsp; And this fact gave the Warg its foothold in history, allowed it to grow to its legendary form. It has since taken root in our imaginations as a terrible beast that feasts on hearts and brains. This is not wrong, and is the primary reason that a Warg is confused with a Worg. One eats your heart and your brain (or prefers to, if the choice is offered), while the other consumes your heartmind. The first is a death of your body, the second a death of the disquiet within it.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>The Worg was discovered after Little War 3. No, 4. It was in Little War 4 when the Jaw of the Skull was finally closed and the two worlds were separated, which led to the unintended consequence of the disentanglement of the heartmind. Before that time, there were no thinking peoples and feeling peoples, no influences of above and below, no disharmony to regulate between the two. There was one continuous thingliness of chaos and order ripping into and out of one another. Pure heartmind governed the Skull for a thousand ages. Then it split, and people began to change. They drifted further into pure heartless reason or absolute animal tempestry. The former took to populating the Overskull, and the later sequestered their brutish ways below. But elements of both the heart and the mind persisted in each. Skullmages first recognized this, noting the inexplicable rise in familial violence and intergenerational conflict throughout the lands. Little War 7 gave way to Large War 1, then Larger War 2, then Largest War 8, until Maximum War 12 threatened to destroy everything in known existence (except for Meatlund, because not even vicious warrior clans want to go to Meatlund). All was almost lost, until the Worg.</p><p><br></p><p>It was a name given to the old god of that strange religion, whose horns were mountain peaks and whose mouth was a volcano. Those antiquarian peoples would scribe their collective feelings onto protean tablets made of massive pond leaves dried in the sun. They would take those scribblings and toss them into the heart of the burning volcano, which they believed would relieve their heartmind of its anguish. If that did not work, they would simply throw themselves in. But, reportedly, it would normally work, and these peoples came to refer to the god as the Thought-Eater, or, in their language, the Woor-Riga. This was the thing that they could use to take the pieces out of their mind and expel them into a more powerful form. A form that could not only destroy the disharmony of the thought, but do so in a way that they all shared with one another.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>This was what the Skullmages brought forward in their design of the Worg, which they gave as a less unwieldy diminution of the original godname. They began to administer it to our own class immediately, and barring certain exceptional cases where the heartmind was spurred into incredible furies, the tool worked spectacularly. Skullmages took to it so much that they enhanced the methodology with non-material writing means, so that they did not have to halt any experiments or studies to exorcise a thought. Soon they began to advocate for its distribution throughout the Overskull, and it brought us to today. Children are taught to incorporate a Worg into their daily habits as soon as they have the words to describe what they are feeling inside. A Worg is as common to the basic maintenance of a human life as soap.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>And yet, the people in this society still get confused between a Warg and a Worg. Perhaps I ought to call it out explicitly in the Overbook. I know that I promised that I wouldn't create any more new editions this year, but one edit to a single entry won't break that pledge.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h1 data-scene-id=\"67543baf-4cbc-4915-b33d-1855412d30d8\">Cretinus of Dredst - Prologus (ex post facto): On the Meaning of the Words</h1><p><b><i><br></i></b></p><p><b><i><br></i></b></p><p><b><i>From the Overbook of Meaning by Cretinus of Dredst. 373rd edition. Volume 2.&nbsp;</i></b></p><p><br></p><p><b><i>Warg [v-AHR-g]</i></b></p><p><i>1. [noun] A great beast; of the genus of canis indigenous to the Underskull.</i></p><p><b><i><br></i></b></p><p><b><i>Worg [w-OOR-g]</i></b></p><p><i>1) [noun] An externalized linguistic device designed to capture a heartmind's expression.</i></p><p><i>2) [verb] To write, or otherwise exorcise an inner anguish.&nbsp;</i></p><p><i>3) [noun][archaic] The Thought-Eater; a primordial god of the old religion.&nbsp;</i></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h1 data-scene-id=\"b7757449-40f3-4e23-b7f6-a7c4c99c49ed\">Ben - I am the love of my life</h1><p><br></p><p>Let me tell you a story of a time before now, when I had a little bit of hope. I was still a prisoner, yes, but in the bonds of an orphanage. There were some two million children in old Orphilon, capital of Orphilund. <i>The city where no one is born. </i>What an outstanding achievement for their civic society. Of course, it isn't true. I saw dozens of women, young and old, give birth to screaming babies that they quickly tossed to the side. Every child older than three knew how to swaddle and assuage a newborn pup. That was life in old Orphilon.&nbsp;</p><p>I came there as a teenager. A poor skulldugger dropped me from his mulecart on the way to the Fellfall mountain and its riches. I'd been his cook for a year while he journeyed from plot to plot trying to strike up a burial ground. He liked his three eggs very particularly: one scrambled, one hard, and one runny for his toast. It was a long time of learning how to cook them all separately and so timely that they all came out hot on his plate in the same minute. Luckily, the ends were all the man cared about, so when I saved time by boiling his egg in his coffee pot, he did not complain. Still, I was not useful to his bone mining, not then. Now'days they put me up for hard labor at every turn, but then I was small.&nbsp;</p><p>That small boy settled into old Orphilon and lived in a sixteener on the little road that bisected the sewer network underneath the administer's castle. It was a sliver of a room, true to its name, exactly one-sixteenth of a standard room, cut with paper walls into equilateral segments, but it was all to myself. I had one stray pillow that I curled into each night with a dogskin coat I'd bilked off one of the Orphaners to keep me warm. There was a lard lamp in the cornern for heat when I could afford it, and an old, dry piece of salver's leaf hanging from the ceiling to help with the stink.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>","meta":{"totalEarned":{"earned":376,"mark":384,"outlineMark":0},"buildOutSeen":["skillCheckReveal","strikeReveal","slaughterReveal","howlReveal","dungeonReveal"],"buildOutRemoved":["solstice"],"buildOutSelected":["dungeon","charms"],"codexCheatCodesSeen":["skullEscapeReveal","worgQuestStoryReveal"],"outline":{"scenes":{},"characters":{},"parts":{}}},"schemaVersion":1}